            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "SAMAGGIE"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I followed the recipe exactly. It was very tasty and easy to make. However, you need to strain it before drinking, as the blackberry seeds are impossible to ignore. Next time I'll substitute something else for the blackberries."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Linda Schanhals"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Almost like mine. But I cook the blackberries and raspberries to soften and put through a strainer to remove the seeds and have a puree. Freeze 1st until they are ice cubes and only use 1/2 banana. I use organic cane sugar instead of honey if needed, because I'm not a honey fan. If it is too thick I add some V8 fusion Goji raspberry or Acai raspberry juice. That juice also adds some veggies to it as well.\r\n\r\nThe puree and some fresh crushed berries also works when making a yogurt parfait with whole grain granola cereal and walnuts.\r\n\r\n1 layer of yogurt your choice\r\n1 layer crushed fruit and the a little puree. Again your choice of fruit\r\n1 layer cereal and walnuts.\r\n\r\nRepeat until the glass of your choice is full.",
            "datePublished": "2011-08-27T11:14:17.307Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jennifer_Cote"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is pretty good, but I tried freezing slices of banana (layered on a plastic bag) first. Didn't even need the honey and the sweetened yogurt- the frozen bananas made it thick and rich! I added some almond milk to make it non-dairy. But hey, this other recipe's good too :)",
            "datePublished": "2010-08-09T08:56:12.143Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Laurie Jamieson Frithiof"
            },
            "reviewBody": "In place of ice cubes, you can thin this smoothie with any favorite liquid from juice to milk. I had to strain the drink because the drink with seeds was too gritty. Then, it was smooth and wonderful! For a thin smoothie, leave out the banana.",
            "datePublished": "2011-01-29T11:39:04.897Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "naples34102"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I have a Vitamix, so that pretty much took care of the seed problem but I can see how this would be an issue without this workhorse of a blender. So I'm not so sure blackberries, with their huge seeds, are the best choice for smoothies. The flavor was good, but we found the banana just a little distracting for a \"Raspberry Blackberry Smoothie.\" I used frozen berries, so the ice cubes weren't necessary, but a little milk to thin it out was.",
            "datePublished": "2011-05-30T05:22:12.537Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "megg"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great. Used plain yogurt. Seeds add fiber!",
